1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:03,490
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:04,190
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,670 --> 00:00:10,410
My name is Kathryn and today we're going to be helping you find someone to help out around the house.

4
00:00:10,730 --> 00:00:11,070
That's right.

5
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:15,040
So we're looking for someone to help us do some things around the house.

6
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:17,590
So we're going to talk about this more in depth.

7
00:00:18,110 --> 00:00:22,070
But first, let's take a look at one word on vocabulary preview.

8
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,420
Vocabulary preview.

9
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:30,350
So the beginning of today's dialogue has to do with a newspaper ad.

10
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:32,570
We are writing an ad for the newspaper.

11
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:35,130
Now what exactly is a newspaper ad, Marco?

12
00:00:35,270 --> 00:00:35,630
That's right.

13
00:00:35,930 --> 00:00:49,510
So in the newspapers, usually there is a section called the classifieds where people post ads or advertisements or adverts where maybe they're selling their car or they're looking to hire people.

14
00:00:50,230 --> 00:00:52,770
So this is what a newspaper ad is.

15
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:57,600
You're selling something, you're buying something or maybe you want to hire people, etc.

16
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:02,320
It is basically an advertisement in the newspaper to find help.

17
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:03,320
All right.

18
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:07,110
So let's listen to our dialogue and when we come back, we'll be talking more about the words and phrases you've heard.

19
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:14,940
Can you help me write a newspaper ad?

20
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:16,560
Sure.

21
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:18,020
What are you looking to buy or sell?

22
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:21,840
Actually, I want to hire someone to help me around the house.

23
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:23,380
Oh, you want to get a maid?

24
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:27,420
Well, I think it's better if you call her a cleaning lady or domestic help.

25
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:28,960
Okay.

26
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:30,680
So what do you want her to do?

27
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:31,940
Well, let's see.

28
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:37,340
I want her to come in three times a week for a couple of hours to clean the kitchen, bathroom and maybe do some cooking.

29
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:38,340
Got it.

30
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:40,780
And how much do you offer per month?

31
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:42,100
I would pay her hourly.

32
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:43,780
I don't know what the going rate is though.

33
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:49,860
I know for sure it's more than minimum wage, but maybe you should just negotiate with the person that answers the ad.

34
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:50,560
Great.

35
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:51,420
Thanks for your help.

36
00:01:54,630 --> 00:01:55,270
All right, we're back.

37
00:01:55,480 --> 00:02:01,000
So now let's take a look at language takeaway.

38
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:01,330
Language takeaway.

39
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Okay.

40
00:02:03,550 --> 00:02:08,520
So first of all today, Marco, the advertisement is about wanting to hire someone.

41
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:13,040
Now, this is an important verb, especially when we're trying to find help.

42
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:14,090
The verb to hire.

43
00:02:14,390 --> 00:02:14,770
That's right.

44
00:02:15,110 --> 00:02:20,390
So to hire means to give someone work, to offer a contract to someone.

45
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:21,130
Okay.

46
00:02:21,150 --> 00:02:25,010
So my boss hired me three years ago.

47
00:02:25,170 --> 00:02:28,170
That means he gave me a job three years ago.

48
00:02:28,330 --> 00:02:28,590
That's right.

49
00:02:28,790 --> 00:02:30,250
But this is not just for office jobs.

50
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:31,890
This is for all kinds of jobs.

51
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:36,920
So what about cutting your grass or doing your lawn?

52
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:37,070
That's right.

53
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:42,030
So you can hire a gardener, for example, and it's basically the same thing.

54
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:45,690
It means that you're going to pay someone to work for you.

55
00:02:46,150 --> 00:02:46,450
Okay.

56
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:52,080
So let's say you wanted to hire a maid, M-A-I-D, a maid.

57
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:54,890
But this is a little bit old fashioned, this word.

58
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:57,720
What does a maid do or what did a maid do?

59
00:02:57,850 --> 00:03:04,970
Well, a maid is basically a woman, a female servant, so to say, a person that basically serves you.

60
00:03:05,370 --> 00:03:05,570
All right.

61
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:07,030
So she cleans the house.

62
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:15,360
She maybe puts the covers on the beds, washes the dishes, cooks, does all of these domestic things.

63
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:16,900
So she works around the house.

64
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:21,680
But because there's a bad association, we don't like to think, oh, we have servants.

65
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:23,900
We don't say maid anymore.

66
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:26,460
We have two other ways of saying this word.

67
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:26,980
That's right.

68
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:31,290
So we could say that we want to hire a cleaning lady, right?

69
00:03:31,490 --> 00:03:33,650
Or we want to hire domestic help.

70
00:03:34,210 --> 00:03:34,590
Okay.

71
00:03:34,630 --> 00:03:36,710
Now there's an important difference between these two words.

72
00:03:37,010 --> 00:03:42,990
First of all, cleaning lady is a woman, a lady, and she is someone who only cleans.

73
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:45,370
She doesn't take care of your children.

74
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:47,520
She doesn't walk your dogs.

75
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:52,650
She basically just cleans the house, washes the dishes, cleans the clothes, washes the floors.

76
00:03:53,250 --> 00:03:54,190
So she's a cleaning lady.

77
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:58,120
Now domestic help, this is a little bit more general.

78
00:03:58,520 --> 00:03:58,680
Right.

79
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:04,660
So domestic help would be a little bit more in depth of what the person's going to do.

80
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:09,420
So we'll help with the domestic work, with domestic meaning the house.

81
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:19,880
So this person will maybe cook for you, clean the house, take care of the kids, do everything that is associated with the house.

82
00:04:20,350 --> 00:04:25,030
So it's important to see that domestic means something that has to do with the house.

83
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:28,560
This is help man or woman in the house.

84
00:04:29,230 --> 00:04:29,350
Right.

85
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:30,560
All right.

86
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:38,360
So that's all we have for language takeaway, but we do have another section, Fluency Builder, in which we're going to look at some great words and phrases.

87
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:38,610
Fluency Builder.

88
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:41,840
All right.

89
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:50,170
So first on Fluency Builder, let's take a look at how we're going to pay the person because obviously we were saying that we want to hire this person.

90
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:51,130
We're going to pay them.

91
00:04:51,490 --> 00:04:52,990
So how are we going to pay them?

92
00:04:53,730 --> 00:04:57,230
The person said, I would pay the woman her hourly.

93
00:04:57,650 --> 00:04:58,410
I would pay her hourly.

94
00:04:58,880 --> 00:04:59,180
Okay.

95
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:00,380
So this is the rate.

96
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:02,200
This is how much she will be paid.

97
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:06,920
And we don't say she will be paid $10,000 per year.

98
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:09,160
We say she'll be paid $10 per hour.

99
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,120
So we pay per hour.

100
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:13,440
This is paying her hourly.

101
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:15,040
Right.

102
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:20,320
So if you have an hourly wage, that means that you get paid per hour.

103
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:26,280
In contrast with a salary, that means that you would get a fixed amount every month.

104
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:26,700
All right.

105
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:34,580
So if the monthly salary is $1,000, for example, you could break that down and say, but every day you earn 100.

106
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,040
You work 10 days.

107
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:40,540
But if it's hourly, you say you'll get paid $10 per hour.

108
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:43,220
So if you work four hours today, you will earn $40.

109
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:44,300
That's right.

110
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:45,240
Okay.

111
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:52,640
So now we want to pay the cleaning lady hourly, but we don't know what the going rate is.

112
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:54,120
Going rate.

113
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:55,480
Going.

114
00:05:55,800 --> 00:06:03,640
So this is an interesting phrase and it's important to notice that it's not going rate is an amount of money, but it's not always the same.

115
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:05,120
Right.

116
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:05,560
It depends on the industry.

117
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:15,920
So the going rate for a teacher is different from the going rate for a cleaning lady, which is different from the going rate for a language tutor.

118
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:16,140
Right.

119
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:18,700
Or like an English tutor.

120
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:26,000
So first of all, the going rate is how much these people, these cleaning ladies normally earn.

121
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:27,000
It's an average.

122
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Right.

123
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:34,120
So you would usually calculate it, say the approximate going rate is between maybe $8 to $10 per hour.

124
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:36,520
So it's not like an exact amount.

125
00:06:36,520 --> 00:06:39,640
You would say the going rate is more or less a certain amount.

126
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:40,460
It's not exact.

127
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:44,080
So you might ask your friends, what do you pay your cleaning lady?

128
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:47,300
And one friend says, oh, I pay $12 per hour.

129
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:50,900
And my other friend says, oh, I pay about $8 per hour.

130
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:56,000
So to me, it sounds like the going rate is about $10 per hour.

131
00:06:56,440 --> 00:06:57,440
Right.

132
00:06:57,440 --> 00:06:58,440
Exactly.

133
00:06:58,440 --> 00:06:59,500
So we're not really sure what the going rate is.

134
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:01,380
So our friend says, well, you know what?

135
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:04,520
I'm sure it's more than minimum wage.

136
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:12,120
Now minimum wage is actually something that the government decides on or either the federal government in America or the state.

137
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:20,320
And each state will have a number that they say that is the least you can pay a person per hour.

138
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:21,320
Right.

139
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:22,320
Right.

140
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:23,320
So it is the law.

141
00:07:23,320 --> 00:07:25,440
If you pay less, it's illegal.

142
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:28,140
And this is because we expect everyone to be treated fairly.

143
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:29,760
Right.

144
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:30,760
Right.

145
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:31,760
So it's not an advantage of people.

146
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:32,000
No.

147
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:38,200
And so, for example, in America, in my state, Illinois, I think the minimum wage is $7.25 per hour.

148
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:38,640
Oh, really?

149
00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:40,840
Wow.

150
00:07:40,840 --> 00:07:41,840
It's pretty high.

151
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:44,800
And this is because people need to pay for health care and cars and houses and children.

152
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:49,640
So we say the least you can pay someone is $7.25.

153
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:50,340
Now this is different.

154
00:07:50,640 --> 00:07:53,120
As you said, it could be different from state to state.

155
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:53,840
Right.

156
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:59,840
Because I know in some states, if I'm not mistaken, it's like $5.50 or something.

157
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:01,520
Yeah, some states it's $5.

158
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:03,370
It depends on the cost of living.

159
00:08:03,670 --> 00:08:11,930
If it's cheap to rent an apartment or to find transportation, then obviously the minimum wage is going to be lower.

160
00:08:12,770 --> 00:08:14,430
Obviously there's no maximum wage.

161
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,960
There's no number that is the most you can pay someone.

162
00:08:17,960 --> 00:08:19,920
But all states have a minimum wage.

163
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:23,880
It's the least that you can pay someone per hour.

164
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:24,880
Okay.

165
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:32,000
So for our last phrase, we don't know what the minimum wage is, we're not really sure what the going rate is.

166
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,900
So we should just negotiate with the person that answers the ad.

167
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:37,740
All right.

168
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:39,820
Now to negotiate is a great verb.

169
00:08:40,840 --> 00:08:42,940
It is something that all of us do all the time.

170
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:49,480
So to negotiate means to try and compromise or to reach an agreement.

171
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:53,630
So maybe I say the most I will pay is $11.

172
00:08:55,170 --> 00:08:57,370
So I say, okay, I'll pay you $9.

173
00:08:57,960 --> 00:09:00,150
And I say, no, I'll take 12.

174
00:09:01,250 --> 00:09:02,910
Yeah, sorry, 12 is too much.

175
00:09:03,210 --> 00:09:04,350
I'll pay you 10.

176
00:09:05,070 --> 00:09:05,880
Okay, 11.

177
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:06,880
11?

178
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:07,880
Okay, agreed.

179
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:08,880
Okay.

180
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:19,240
So it's a way of reaching an agreement, like you say, where both sides have their goals and they try to find a price that is the best for both parties.

181
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:19,400
That's right.

182
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:27,280
So it's basically a verb to negotiate and this is what you do, as you said, in many circumstances, especially when you're getting a job.

183
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:28,560
Yes.

184
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:29,560
All right.

185
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:38,920
So let's listen to the dialogue one last time and we'll be back in a bit.

186
00:09:38,920 --> 00:09:40,160
Can you help me write a newspaper ad?

187
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:41,060
Sure.

188
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:43,240
What are you looking to buy or sell?

189
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:46,480
Actually, I want to hire someone to help me around the house.

190
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:48,580
Oh, you want to get a maid?

191
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:52,640
Well, I think it's better if you call her a cleaning lady or domestic help.

192
00:09:53,730 --> 00:09:54,130
Okay.

193
00:09:54,410 --> 00:09:55,970
So what do you want her to do?

194
00:09:56,450 --> 00:09:57,250
Well, let's see.

195
00:09:57,770 --> 00:10:02,590
I want her to come in three times a week for a couple of hours to clean the kitchen, bathroom, and maybe do some cooking.

196
00:10:03,050 --> 00:10:03,650
Got it.

197
00:10:04,030 --> 00:10:06,090
And how much do you offer per month?

198
00:10:06,530 --> 00:10:07,430
I would pay her hourly.

199
00:10:07,760 --> 00:10:09,720
I don't know what the going rate is, though.

200
00:10:09,720 --> 00:10:15,150
I know for sure it's more than minimum wage, but maybe you should just negotiate with the person that answers the ad.

201
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:15,870
Great.

202
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:20,040
I'll be happy to help.

203
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:21,040
All right.

204
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:27,350
So talking about minimum wage, is there any exception to why a person would earn less than minimum wage?

205
00:10:27,730 --> 00:10:28,990
I don't believe so.

206
00:10:29,190 --> 00:10:31,950
Well, if they're not legally employed, right?

207
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:39,760
So if you're talking about people who we say are under the table, working under the table, that means that they're not working legally.

208
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:40,620
They're not paying taxes.

209
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:42,820
Then maybe those people will be paid less.

210
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:44,960
What about waiters and waitresses?

211
00:10:45,180 --> 00:10:49,040
I've heard that they earn less than minimum wage, but because they earn tips.

212
00:10:49,620 --> 00:10:50,060
This is true.

213
00:10:50,760 --> 00:11:02,240
So most restaurants say, okay, we will pay you as a waiter $3 an hour, but because you will earn tips, you will earn more than the minimum wage.

214
00:11:02,240 --> 00:11:03,240
Right.

215
00:11:03,240 --> 00:11:03,740
So this is a good exception.

216
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:06,160
Unfortunately, there's no promise.

217
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:10,120
If you have no customers, you earn no tips, so you're still earning less than minimum wage.

218
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:11,960
That's right.

219
00:11:11,960 --> 00:11:19,360
But if you have many, many customers and you earn lots of tips and you make $15 an hour or something much higher than the minimum wage.

220
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:19,480
That's right.

221
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:30,080
And actually being a waiter or a waitress is a very common job that a lot of people have when they're still in school or over the summer.

222
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:35,850
And I've heard that it could be quite good because Americans are pretty good tippers.

223
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:36,570
That's right.

224
00:11:37,440 --> 00:11:39,930
Americans generally tip between 15 and 20%.

225
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:45,080
And you know that you have to tip because your waiter does not earn a minimum wage.

226
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:46,080
Right.

227
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:47,570
And so I think there is this expectation.

228
00:11:48,510 --> 00:11:55,740
So if your bill comes out to like a hundred bucks, then you are most definitely going to leave at least a $20 tip.

229
00:11:56,020 --> 00:11:59,900
Usually, yeah, 15 to $20, $20 if it's that much money.

230
00:12:00,140 --> 00:12:00,480
Wow.

231
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:02,320
Wow.

232
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:12,920
That's very, very interesting, especially considering that in many countries, tipping is not really considered, it's not expected or it's not really something that people do.

233
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:13,180
Exactly.

234
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:16,320
And in America, it is something that you have to do.

235
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:20,340
So I think a lot of times when people come to America, they don't expect to pay this.

236
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:21,840
There's tax, there's tip.

237
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:27,040
So when you see a menu, remember that the cost is a lot higher than you see.

238
00:12:27,040 --> 00:12:27,220
Exactly.

239
00:12:28,040 --> 00:12:29,040
All right.

240
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:29,660
Very, very interesting topic.

241
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:33,780
Let us know if you have any questions, any doubts or comments.

242
00:12:33,780 --> 00:12:35,740
You can find us at EnglishPod.com.

243
00:12:35,740 --> 00:12:36,360
We'll see you guys there.

244
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:37,740
Bye.

245
00:12:37,740 --> 00:12:45,362
Bye.
